

PTY(4)               UNIX Programmer's Manual                PTY(4)



NAME
     pty - pseudo terminal driver

SYNOPSIS
     /dev/ptyp0, /dev/ptyp1        pseudo-terminal masters
     /dev/ttyp0, /dev/ttyp1        pseudo-terminal slaves

DESCRIPTION
     The pty driver provides support for a device-pair termed a
     pseudo terminal.  A pseudo terminal is a pair of character
     devices, a master and a slave.  The slave device provides an
     interface identical to that described in tty(4); anything
     written on the master appears as input to the slave, and
     anything written on the slave is presented as input on the
     master.

     The slave is a terminal in every respect that matters: it has
     a line discipline, an erase and kill character, a foreground
     process group, and a window size.  A program reading it
     cannot tell it from a real line, which is the point --
     telnetd(8) and script-like tools put a shell on one and read
     the other end.

     The master's presence IS carrier.  A slave whose master is
     not open reads end-of-file; when the master closes, the
     slave's session is hung up (SIGHUP followed by SIGCONT) and
     both queues are flushed.  Opening a master that is already
     open fails with EBUSY: a pty pair has one controller.

     FGX provides TWO pairs, numbered 0 and 1.

IOCTLS
     The standard tty(4) ioctls are answered on the SLAVE, which
     is the terminal.  In addition:

     TIOCPKT  Set or clear "packet mode" on the MASTER.  With
              packet mode enabled, each read from the master
              returns a status byte first.  A read that returns
              data has TIOCPKT_DATA (zero) at the head; a read
              that returns a status alone returns exactly one
              byte, which is one of

                   TIOCPKT_FLUSHREAD    input was flushed
                   TIOCPKT_FLUSHWRITE   output was flushed
                   TIOCPKT_STOP         output was stopped
                   TIOCPKT_START        output was restarted

              A status byte is never followed by data in the same
              read.  telnetd(8) uses this to relay the session's
              flow control to the far end.

     TIOCSTOP, TIOCSTART
              Stop and restart the slave's output, as if the user
              had typed the stop and start characters.  Issued on
              the slave.

     TIOCOUTQ Answers the number of characters queued for output
              on the slave and not yet read by the master.

DIAGNOSTICS
     None; the driver reports failures through the usual errno
     values.  EIO from a slave write means the master has gone;
     EBUSY from a master open means one is already there.

SEE ALSO
     tty(4), telnetd(8), stty(1)

BUGS
     REMOTE MODE IS NOT IMPLEMENTED.  4.2BSD and later provide a
     TIOCREMOTE ioctl on the master which puts the pair into
     "remote and flow controlled input" mode: the master writes
     whole lines directly into the slave's canonical queue,
     bypassing the line discipline, and each write(2) becomes one
     record.  It exists so that a master which does its own line
     editing -- a login daemon in LINEMODE, classically -- is not
     edited a second time locally.

     FGX refuses TIOCREMOTE with ENOTTY rather than accepting it
     and doing nothing, which would leave a caller believing its
     next write had gone in as a record while the bytes were
     edited by the very discipline remote mode exists to bypass.

     The obstacle is not effort.  Upstream marks the end of each
     remote write with a NUL sentinel appended after the last
     byte, and the slave's read drains up to it -- so a record
     boundary is required.  FGX's character-device write is a
     per-byte loop, and no byte in such a loop knows that it is
     the last one.  Giving the master a bulk write entry would not
     settle it either: that entry serves the /dev vnode route,
     while the system-call route a pty master actually uses stays
     per-byte.  Remote mode therefore waits on a record boundary
     that the write path does not have for ANY character device.

     Nothing in FGX asks for it today.  telnetd(8) runs the
     session a character at a time and lets the slave's own
     discipline do the editing.

     The pairs are fixed at two and their names are fixed with
     them; there is no pty allocation service, so a program that
     wants a pty opens the masters in turn until one does not
     answer EBUSY.
